   The fact that the source shows the AC being randomized is misleading!!
I guess you just can't trust people to read source. :=)  In fact what
the outcome of this function turns out to be is a function which
makes going from AC 3 to AC 2 to be better than going from AC -2 to AC -3.
The fact that the function randomizes on the AC is not what is
important.  What is important is the creature's chance to hit!  Try the
following:


   The function:
> /*      Work out the armor class differential   */
>         tmp = u.uac + 10;               /* tmp ~= 0 - 20 */
> /*      give people with Ac < -9 at least some vulnerability */
> /*      negative AC gives an actual AC of somewhere from -1 to the AC */
>         if (tmp < 10) tmp = 10 - rnd(10-tmp);
>         tmp += mtmp->m_lev;
>         if(multi < 0) tmp += 4;
>         if((Invis && !perceives(mdat)) || !mtmp->mcansee)
>                 tmp -= 2;
>         if(mtmp->mtrapped) tmp -= 2;
>         if(tmp <= 0) tmp = 1;

  Now start out with a monster level of say 5 and works out the chances to
get hit with the following AC's: 10, 5, 0, -1, -5, -10, -20

AC    10     5     0    -1    -5   -10   -20
+10   20    15    10     9     5     0   -10

rnd   20    15    10     9   5-9   0-9  -10-9
mtmp  25    20    15    14  10-14  5-14 -5-14

Assuming no effect for multi & Invis & Trapped) this number is then
set to 1 if it is less than 1 (There is always a 5% chance)
  Now lets calculate the % chance of being hit. The only tricky ones
are the variable ones.  They should be done by summing their
chance of being hit for each of the numbers * the chance of each number 
being chosen.  (i.e. for -5  we have 10/20 *1/5 + 11/20 * 1/5 + ... +
14/20 * 1/5 = 60/20 * 1/5 = 60%)  Remember to change a 0/20 or
negative number to 1/20 before doing this calculation.  Now for the 
results:
AC    10     5     0    -1    -5   -10   -20
mtmp  25    20    15    14  10-14  5-14 -5-14
hit 100%  100%   75%   70%   60%  47.5% 27.75%

   It will approach but never quite reach the 5% minimum.  This is
what one poster suggested as a solution (always decreasing but at
a continually slower pace.)  I think the creator of the function
did a great job of this.  The creators only problem what not commenting
what the outcome of the function looked like.  (Must have assumed that
noone would jump to conclusions)

   Please forgive any math errors and remember the 1 minimum (i.e. for
the AC -20 ,  % = (14/20 + 13/20 + ... + 2/20 + 1/20 + 1/20 + 1/20 +
1/20 + 1/20 + 1/20 + 1/20) * 1/20 )

